WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.639
This one's optional.

2
00:00:00.639 --> 00:00:07.000
If you'd rather audit the code yourself, avoid the unsigned binary, or you want to develop on the app, we'll build HarnessStation from source — and set up the dev loop the developer track uses later.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:16.064
You need three things installed first: Node, version eighteen or newer; Rust, through rustup; and a C++ toolchain — that's the Visual Studio 2022 Build Tools with the C++ workload on Windows, or build-essential on Linux.

4
00:00:16.064 --> 00:00:18.000
The C++ part trips people up, so don't skip it.

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.470
Clone the repo, then install the JavaScript dependencies with npm install.

6
00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:27.000
This pulls the front-end packages; the Rust crates come on the first build.

7
00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.550
Now run the app in dev mode with npm run tauri dev.

8
00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:36.000
Fair warning: the very first build compiles several hundred Rust crates and takes a few minutes — don't panic when it sits there.

9
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:40.000
After that it's incremental and fast, and the front end hot-reloads as you edit.

10
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.620
When you want a real installer — the same kind we downloaded in the earlier episodes — run npm run tauri build.

11
00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:48.000
It produces the packaged app for your platform.

12
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:55.463
Two commands worth knowing, and a nice credibility check: npm test runs the project's real test suite — hundreds of tests — and npx tsc --noEmit type-checks the whole thing.

13
00:00:55.463 --> 00:00:57.145
If both are green, your build is sound.

14
00:00:57.145 --> 00:00:59.000
We'll lean on these in the developer track.

15
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:05.000
However you installed it, next we look at where the app keeps your data on disk — before you put anything into it — so nothing that follows feels like a black box.

